<p>Sweden says it found and neutralised a drone operating over Swedish waters near the French aircraft carrier Charles de Gaulle while the ship was in the area, and later confirmed the drone was Russian. Swedish authorities called the episode serious but not unexpected, saying it fits behaviour they have seen from Russia elsewhere. France condemned the incident as a “ridiculous provocation,” while Russia rejected the allegation as “absurd”. Sweden’s forces said they used electronic measures to end the incident and that the carrier was not in danger at any point.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Op4U2kcJO8iq6BWMsPWWRWUdDyLdfUAJoNWkGNvmG_fAX9ZkApq32hy7--fRApba3ZKz5Us-7rTEvrO6DDfyT6cUCMvQP5yBedwTmtCdVgrc_dyStcoV8VvS0js2wB4X2yjHdR9ArzOYvM_0Ex5aevyZr4sF1yOQC6qwBwucchrExm6i1aArKB-lSrwwMLcsy57P9pVVosT-lszqwnp-SGd37rMVbPwIfuSUIWsFSdBAEi2xFxX4tjHfJQ' alt='French Foreign Minister Jean-Noel Barrot speaks at a press conference in Sweden, Friday Feb. 27, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Electronic response: Sweden’s military said it used jamming to stop the drone and neutralised it over Swedish waters.</li><li>Carrier safety: The Swedish Navy said the Charles de Gaulle was not in danger at any point during the incident.</li><li>Diplomatic sparring: France denounced the flight as a “ridiculous provocation,” while Russia called the accusation “absurd”.</li><li>Security backdrop: Sweden’s prime minister said the incident was serious but “not unexpected,” describing it as a familiar Russian pattern of behaviour.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sweden (government): Prime Minister Ulf Kristersson said the incident was serious but not unexpected, and described it as a recognised Russian way of operating. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1mj3e2nz8ko'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>France (government): French officials condemned what they described as a suspected surveillance attempt as a “ridiculous provocation”. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/internationa